|~ `::vec`, the kind of vectors, the refusal of its constructor,
   and the verbs of vectors. Each resides on `::qlang/vec`, the kind
   whose module declares it, under the contract of its name on
   `::qlang/any`, whose page and laws it answers; a quote reads as
   the vector of its steps. The head of each is its declaration, which
   the runtime executes before the primitive runs. ~|

:VecPayloadNotVecError
  |~~ `::vec` reads a vector, a set or a quote as the bare vector; a
      payload of another kind is refused.
      ~(
        ::vec{:a 1}
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/VecPayloadNotVecError ::map]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:count
  |~~ The number of elements.

      ~([1 2 3] | count | eq 3)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/count})

:empty
  |~~ Whether the vector holds no element.

      ~([] | empty | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/empty})

:first
  |~~ The first element, or null for the empty vector.

      ~([10 20 30] | first | eq 10)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/first})

:last
  |~~ The last element, or null for the empty vector.

      ~([10 20 30] | last | eq 30)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/last})

:sum
  |~~ The sum of the elements, each a number.

      ~([1 2 3 4] | sum | eq 10)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/sum})

:avg
  |~~ The mean of the elements, each a number, or null when there is none.

      ~([1 2 3 4] | avg | eq 2.5)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/avg})

:min
  |~~ The first element in the one order of values, or the one whose key
      answers the first value.

      ~([3 1 4 1 5] | min | eq 1)
      ~([{:age 30} {:age 20}] | min ~(/age) | eq {:age 20})
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:key #[::quote ::null] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/min})

:max
  |~~ The last element in the one order of values, or the one whose key
      answers the last value.

      ~([3 1 4 1 5] | max | eq 5)
      ~([{:age 30} {:age 40}] | max ~(/age) | eq {:age 40})
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:key #[::quote ::null] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/max})

:filter
  |~~ The elements whose predicate answers true, in their order.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | filter ~(gt 2) | eq [3 4 5])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:predicate ::quote | :returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/filter})

:every
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for every element.

      ~([2 4 6 8] | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:predicate ::quote | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/every})

:any
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for some element.

      ~([1 2 3] | any ~(gt 2) | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:predicate ::quote | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/any})

:has
  |~~ Whether the vector holds the element, an element equal to it; the
      membership of a JSON array.

      ~(["ops" "db"] | has "ops" | eq true)
      ~([1 [2]] | has [2] | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:element ::any | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/has})

:contains
  |~~ Whether the vector holds an element equal to the value, the
      membership `has` asks, under the word a string answers for its
      substrings.

      ~(["ops" "db"] | contains "ops" | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:element ::any | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/has})

:groupBy
  |~~ The map of vectors the key sorts the elements into. A key of a
      map is a keyword, and a key that answers a string, the field of a
      JSON object, or a number files its element under the keyword of
      its text.

      ~([{:k :a :v 1} {:k :b :v 2} {:k :a :v 3}] | groupBy ~(/k) | /a * /v | eq [1 3])
      ~([{:s "paid"} {:s "open"} {:s "paid"}] | groupBy ~(/s) * count | eq {:paid 2 :open 1})

      Without a key an element files under itself, so the occurrences
      of each value are counted by `groupBy * count`:
      ~(["b" "a" "b"] | groupBy * count | eq {:b 2 :a 1})
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:key #[::quote ::null] | :returns ::map | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/groupBy})

:indexBy
  |~~ The map of the elements under the key each answers, the last
      element winning a key two share; a key that answers a string is
      made a keyword first, as for `groupBy`.

      ~([{:id :a :n 1} {:id :b :n 2}] | indexBy ~(/id) | /b/n | eq 2)
      ~(["a" "b"] | indexBy | eq {:a "a" :b "b"})
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:key #[::quote ::null] | :returns ::map | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/indexBy})

:sort
  |~~ The elements in the one order of values, the way a reader ranks
      what a query found.

      Numbers ascend, equal ones side by side:
      ~([3 1 4 1 5] | sort | eq [1 1 3 4 5])
      A key orders by what it answers for each element:
      ~([{:age 30} {:age 20}] | sort ~(/age) | eq [{:age 20} {:age 30}])
      A key under `desc` descends, and a vector key breaks the ties by its
      next place:
      ~([{:age 30} {:age 40}] | sort ~(/age | desc) | first | /age | eq 40)
      ~([{:n 1 :s "b"} {:n 2 :s "c"} {:n 2 :s "a"}] | sort ~([(/n | desc) /s]) * /s | eq ["a" "c" "b"])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:key #[::quote ::null] | :returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/sort})

:take
  |~~ The first count elements.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | take 3 | eq [1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:count ::number | :returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/take})

:drop
  |~~ The elements after the first count.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | drop 2 | eq [3 4 5])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:count ::number | :returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/drop})

:at
  |~~ The element at the index, a negative one counting from the end,
      or null past either end.

      ~([10 20 30] | at 1 | eq 20)
      ~([10 20 30] | at -1 | eq 30)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:index ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/at})

:distinct
  |~~ The set of the elements, the vector in the one order without
      duplicates.

      ~([2 1 2 3 1] | distinct | eq #[1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/distinct})

:reverse
  |~~ The elements in the reverse order.

      ~([1 2 3] | reverse | eq [3 2 1])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/reverse})

:flat
  |~~ The elements with one level of nesting spliced in.

      ~([[1 2] [3] [4 5]] | flat | eq [1 2 3 4 5])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/flat})

:reduce
  |~~ The seed folded over the elements by the reducer.

      ~([1 2 3 4 5] | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 15)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:seed ::any | :reducer ::quote | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/reduce})

:union
  |~~ The union of the operands the vector holds, sets merged in the one
      order or maps merged with the last value winning a key. A vector is
      no set for the algebra, so another operand beside it is
      refused with the pair.

      ~([#[:a :b] #[:b :c]] | union | eq #[:a :b :c])
      ~([1] | union #[2] !| type | eq ::vec/UnionPairIncompatibleError)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:with #[::set ::map ::null] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/union})

:UnionBareEmptyError
  |~~ `union` over a vector folds its operands, so the vector holds one
      at least.
      ~(
        [] | union
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::vec/UnionBareEmptyError]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:UnionPairIncompatibleError
  |~~ The operands `union` folds are two sets or two maps.
      ~(
        [#[:a] {:b 1}] | union
        !| [type /leftType /rightType]
        |  eq [::vec/UnionPairIncompatibleError ::set ::map]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:minus
  |~~ The first operand the vector holds minus each one after it; another
      operand beside the vector is refused with the pair.

      ~([#[:a :b :c] #[:b]] | minus | eq #[:a :c])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:with #[::set ::map ::null] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/minus})

:MinusBareEmptyError
  |~~ `minus` over a vector folds its operands, so the vector holds one
      at least.
      ~(
        [] | minus
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::vec/MinusBareEmptyError]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:MinusPairIncompatibleError
  |~~ The operands `minus` folds are two sets, or a map and a map or a
      vector of keys.
      ~(
        [#[:a] {:b 1}] | minus
        !| [type /leftType /rightType]
        |  eq [::vec/MinusPairIncompatibleError ::set ::map]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:inter
  |~~ The intersection of the operands the vector holds; another operand
      beside the vector is refused with the pair.

      ~([#[:a :b :c] #[:b :d]] | inter | eq #[:b])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:with #[::set ::map ::null] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/inter})

:InterBareEmptyError
  |~~ `inter` over a vector folds its operands, so the vector holds one
      at least.
      ~(
        [] | inter
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::vec/InterBareEmptyError]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:InterPairIncompatibleError
  |~~ The operands `inter` folds are two sets, or a map and a map or a
      vector of keys.
      ~(
        [#[:a] {:b 1}] | inter
        !| [type /leftType /rightType]
        |  eq [::vec/InterPairIncompatibleError ::set ::map]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:join
  |~~ The strings the vector holds joined with the separator between
      consecutive ones. Inverse of `split`.

      ~(["a" "b" "c"] | join "," | eq "a,b,c")
      ~([] | join "," | eq "")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:separator ::string | :returns ::string | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/join})

:JoinSubjectNotVecError
  |~~ The subject of `join` is the vector of strings it writes as one; a
      map's values are read first, `vals | join ","`.
      ~(
        42 | join ","
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/JoinSubjectNotVecError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:JoinElementNotStringError
  |~~ Every element `join` writes is a string; `:index` names the first
      that is not, and `* print` makes each value its text first.
      ~(
        ["a" 42 "c"] | join ","
        !| [type /actualType /index]
        |  eq [::vec/JoinElementNotStringError ::number 1]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:JoinSeparatorNotStringError
  |~~ The separator of `join` is a string, `join ", "`.
      ~(
        ["a" "b"] | join 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::vec/JoinSeparatorNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:prepend
  |~~ The vector with the element at its head, any value; a set takes it
      through its constructor, so it stays a set.

      ~([2 3] | prepend 1 | eq [1 2 3])
      ~(#[2 3] | prepend 3 | eq #[2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:element ::any | :returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/prepend})

:append
  |~~ The vector with the element at its end, any value, so `[1] |
      append [2 3]` answers `[1 [2 3]]` and vectors join through
      `flat`; a set takes it through its constructor, so it stays a set.

      ~([1 2] | append 3 | eq [1 2 3])
      ~(#[1 2] | append 1 | eq #[1 2])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:element ::any | :returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/append})
